Description: advertise Active Directory Web Services in the CLDAP netlogon response
 A Windows client looking for a domain controller that speaks ADWS calls
 DsGetDcName with DS_WEB_SERVICE_REQUIRED. The locator keeps only the
 controllers whose CLDAP netlogon response carries DS_SERVER_WEBSERV in
 server_type. Samba never sets that bit, because Samba itself does not
 implement ADWS, so a domain served by this module is invisible to the
 Active Directory Administrative Center and to the ActiveDirectory
 PowerShell module used without an explicit -Server.
 .
 The bit is off unless smb.conf carries "adws:advertise = yes".
 Announcing the capability with nothing listening on 9389 is worse than
 not announcing it: the client locates the controller, connects, and
 fails, instead of looking elsewhere or reporting that no ADWS
 controller exists.
 .
 lpcfg_parm_bool reads a generic parameter, so this needs no entry in the
 parameter table, no documentation change, and no IDL regeneration.
 DS_SERVER_WEBSERV already exists in librpc/idl/netlogon.idl, as does
 NBT_SERVER_ADS_WEB_SERVICE in librpc/idl/nbt.idl; both are 0x00002000.
Author: HardenSamba
Bug: https://github.com/HardenSamba/samba-adcs/issues/20
Forwarded: not-needed
Last-Update: 2026-08-20
--- a/source4/dsdb/samdb/ldb_modules/netlogon.c
+++ b/source4/dsdb/samdb/ldb_modules/netlogon.c
@@ -294,6 +294,15 @@
 		}
 	}

+	/* HardenSamba: advertise Active Directory Web Services when a separate
+	 * ADWS daemon serves 9389. Off unless smb.conf carries
+	 * "adws:advertise = yes", because announcing the capability with nothing
+	 * listening makes a client connect and fail rather than look elsewhere. */
+	if (lpcfg_parm_bool(lp_ctx, NULL, "adws", "advertise", false)) {
+		server_type |= DS_SERVER_WEBSERV;
+	}
+
 	if (dc_level >= DS_DOMAIN_FUNCTION_2012) {
 		server_type |= DS_SERVER_DS_8;
 	}
